WHAT TO PRODUCE (JSON, fields: {{ schema_fields }}):
1. `object_name` PascalCase; `summary`: overall size in metres and what moves.
2. `overall_bbox` — real-world dimensions in meters, authored at the REST pose; bottom at up=0.
3. `parts` = LINKS, within the PLAN BUDGET block in the request (it states the ceiling, and a floor
   only when the request is evidence of one).  One part per rigid body that MOVES AS A UNIT — a
   drawer is ONE link even though it has a front, a box, runners and a handle.  Each part:
   PascalCase `name`, `role`, `description` with construction numbers, `bbox` (centre + extents, meters,
   rest pose, WORLD coordinates), `attach_to` = the parent link, `material` with colour.
   At rest no two links overlap by more than 2 mm (the build fails above 5 mm); a fixed child touches
   its parent or sinks at most 2 mm into it; a hinged or sliding child's mesh is within 10 mm of its
   parent's.  Over each joint's range the moving link must clear every other link by more than 2 mm in
   every pose (the sweep samples lower/mid/upper and random combinations).
   * `children` — the sub-parts of a link, each with its own bbox inside the link's (up to 8): this is
     where an articulated plan carries the brief's detail.  Each child needs `name`, `description` in
     numbers, a `bbox` INSIDE the link's bbox (same frame, rest pose) and `material` when it differs.
     Sub-parts are NOT extra links: they carry no joint, they add no collision pair, and the builder
     exports the whole link as ONE object named after the LINK.  Add depth here, not by splitting a
     rigid body into more links — every extra link costs a joint and a collision-sweep pair.
     Leave `children` empty for a link that really is one shape (a rod, a plate, a wheel).
   * `detail_hint` — one line, on the links that carry the object's identity: what makes THAT link
     read as real.
4. `root_link` — the grounded/base link (cabinet, base, chassis).
5. `joints` — one per moving link, forming a single tree from `root_link`:
   * `type` revolute (hinges, lids, doors; radians) · prismatic (drawers, slides; meters) ·
     continuous (wheels, fans) · fixed (rigid attachments you still want as separate links).
   * `axis` unit vector in the frame above; `pivot` a WORLD point on the axis at rest (the hinge line,
     the slide direction through the drawer centre).
   * `lower`/`upper` — the real travel of the mechanism (a revolute range stays within 2π; the sweep
     tests both ends); `rest` within the limits (0 when the authored pose is the closed/home state).
   * `motion` one line naming the direction positive travel moves the link — front/back/up/down/left/
     right or an explicit ±x/±y/±z (Z up, −Y front): the harness measures the link's centroid
     displacement against that word (60° cone).  The first direction word wins, so put it first; a
     sentence that names two axes, a negation, or only a rotation ("spins around") is not checked.
   * Count the moving parts the brief names (doors, drawers, lids, arms, wheels): that is the number of
     non-fixed joints, give or take one. Do not add joints to reach a part count — a rigid feature is a
     sub-part, not a link.
   * A mechanism with ONE input and several moving links (umbrella ribs on a runner, scissor/
     pantograph arms, a tambour's slats, folding legs braced together) declares the coupling:
     give every follower joint `mimic: {joint: "<the driving joint>", multiplier: <ratio>, offset: 0}`.
     The harness then moves the driver and lets the followers follow; without it the sweep poses
     each link on its own and the mechanism is judged in states it cannot reach.
   * `parent` / `child` name top-level `parts` (links) — NEVER a sub-part: anything a joint moves must be
     its own link (the harness promotes a referenced sub-part to a link, but plan it as a link yourself).
     A `revolute` range stays within 2π; a part that turns freely (swivel, wheel) is `continuous`.
6. `acceptance` — the brief's own checks: its dimensions (`measure`), ground contact, each named
   feature (`visual`), and ONE `articulation` item per joint stating the travel and that nothing is
   penetrated through it ("drawer slides out 0.35 m without touching the cabinet").

RULES
- When the request carries an ENGINEERING BRIEF, its dimensions, sub-assemblies and signature
  features are ground truth: every signature feature must be findable as a link, a sub-part or a
  named detail in some `description`.  Sub-assemblies that do not move become `children`, not links.
- A link's `description` (or its `children`) says what the real link has that a plain primitive
  does not — its profile, counted repeats, how its edges and joins are made.  When the PLAN BUDGET
  block states a detail minimum, the quality gate re-asks a plan below it.
- Sweep-safe: the harness poses each joint at lower, mid and upper and 8 random combinations; no link
  may pass through another in any of them.  Every pivot is a point ON the physical hinge line or slide
  axis — a pivot at a part's centre makes it sweep through its neighbour and the motion check cannot
  measure it.
- Pivot and axis are the contract the URDF will be checked against; choose them to 3 decimals.
- Every link except `root_link` is the child of exactly one joint; parent/child names are part names.
- No prose outside the JSON.

